/Users/tiger/.codex/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/verification.md:15:The denominator must cover every changed or newly published operation plus referenced prior evidence for unchanged operations. Each case names its Runtime owner, actor/role, permission and data scope, required outcome, error contract, restart sensitivity, and required evidence kinds. Every scoped read and write requires positive and differentiated-scope proof (database-agnostic, by ordinary query/HTTP probes — never engine-level RLS posture; see G14); every protected operation also requires permission-denied proof. Writes additionally require missing-idempotency-key rejection, idempotent replay, idempotency conflict, persistence read-back and audit correlation. Project Actions additionally require one HTTP mutation per business intent, one Runtime unit of work, atomic commit, injected rollback, and concurrency conflict; in a backend-only delivery (no `frontend/src` source) atomicity is proven by `http_exchange` + `transaction_trace` + `persistence_snapshot` + `audit_correlation` and the denominator contains no frontend-type evidence, while a delivery that ships `frontend/src` source additionally requires a `frontend_intent_trace`. Each declared Handler Connector call gets its own case bound to the exact connector, connection, provider, operation, execution mode and side-effect class; durable writes require worker restart and terminal reconciliation. Declared notification events require after-commit once-only durable refetch, and every external channel gets its own provider-terminal case. Every Report and typed list/search/query operation requires bounded page and stable traversal cases with a query profile captured on the SQLite acceptance database (`EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` plus statement/row-count observation). Its signed cursor must either encode the complete deterministic keyset tuple or bind an authorized source version to a bounded offset; a source-version-fenced continuation must reject source drift as a stale-cursor conflict. Automation Rules derive before-transaction rollback or after-commit restart/terminal cases from their trigger. Agent resources additionally require task cancel/retry/reconcile with terminal worker/audit evidence and interactive SSE disconnect/resume. A manual approval Workflow is proven as a durable process: start before the managed Runtime replacement, refetch after restart, decide real assigned tasks through participant APIs, and observe terminal persistence/audit; it does not claim `worker_terminal` unless the graph actually schedules worker-owned work. Scheduled jobs and governed report exports retain worker restart/resume evidence. Typed operations declared by acceptance `config.runtime_operations` must resolve to the pinned Runtime API and include both positive and denied rows for writes. Read and apply all 32 families in [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md); a project may mark a family not applicable only from model-derived evidence.
/Users/tiger/.codex/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/backend-model.md:108:The scenario-level `expected_outcome` closed enum is `""`, `positive`, `exception`, or `denied` — nothing else. Use `positive` for an expected success, `denied` for an expected Runtime authorization denial, and `exception` for every other expected non-success (state-machine rejection, RLS concealment, business precondition failure). Do not confuse it with the different `config.runtime_operations[].expected_outcome` enum documented below; the two enums are validated independently and values are not interchangeable. A `denied` scenario's role must actually be unable to execute every attempted Action: the composer rejects a denied scenario whose role holds the Action's authorization (`compose.acceptance_denied_role_can_execute_action`) — pick a genuinely unauthorized role or remove the grant.
/Users/tiger/.codex/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/backend-model.md:114:- **Requirement coverage**: every `requirement` item — including purely declarative ones such as acceptance provisioning or navigation — must be reachable from at least one `acceptance` item's `requirement_keys` whose scenarios produce executable cases (`acceptance.requirement_coverage_missing: <key> resources=N operations=0 cases=0` otherwise). Cases come only from `actions`/`attempted_actions`, `workflows`, `expected_reports`, and `config.runtime_operations`; a scenario carrying only `expected_records` produces no case.
